		"civilopediaText": [ {"text": "Mississippian warfare typically utilized clubs and stone axes for melee weapons. Clubs were a point of pride and skill – the weapon of nobility. Battles appear to have been significant enough that Mississippians built fortifications around major settlements, although these defenses may have been designed to prevent war via intimidation rather than defend against actual siege. \nFor ranged attacks, thick bows held a draw of about fifty pounds (about half the force of an English longbow). Mississippian cities were constructed of wood and thatch. This meant that fire was a devastating weapon against them. Evidence from Cahokia shows that fire arrows were used offensively during raids – the resulting devastation and confusion only increased the shock value of such warfare."}, {"separator": true}],
        "attackSound": "arrow"
