		"civilopediaText": [ {"text": "War elephants – voi chiến in Vietnamese – were a staple of armies across Southeast Asia. Able to move with surprising grace even when hauling heavy loads, they were vital in logistics, and in battle, they were terrifying. From a commanding height, mounted atop tons of hide and flesh, elephant-mounted warriors armed with spears, pikes, or bows could reach above and behind enemy lines, and the beast itself could be goaded into crashing through and trampling even seasoned soldiers. Cavalry, it seems, were particularly susceptible to an elephant charge, as horses required a great deal of discipline and training to keep up an assault when facing a mass of elephants. As such, they were the tanks of their day. \nBut an elephant is, after all, a living thing. In the wars against the Song Dynasty, the forces of Lý Thường Kiệt, emboldened by their success against the Cham to the south, deployed war elephants against the Chinese. However, Chinese forces had learned to attack the sensitive trunks of the animals, causing them to fly into a rage and trample their own forces \nElephants remained in use for their hauling capacity and relative stealth well into the Vietnam War."}, {"separator": true}],
